RESEARCH LOGBOOK

Project: Phase-Space Irreversibility and Stellar Disk Heating in an Idealised Milky Way–Andromeda Merger
Researcher: Abhinav Vatsa
Date: January 21st 2026

⸻

OBJECTIVE

Extend the numerical pipeline for a collisionless Milky Way–Andromeda merger simulation to include trajectory management, irreversibility diagnostics, disk heating metrics, angular momentum transport, and numerical validation, while maintaining physical transparency and modular design.

⸻

WORK COMPLETED
	1.	Integrated all simulation components into a single executable pipeline with consistent phase-space state definitions.
	2.	Implemented snapshot and trajectory management to store time-resolved system states independent of runtime execution.
	3.	Implemented coarse-grained six-dimensional phase-space density estimation and entropy evolution for quantitative irreversibility analysis.
	4.	Implemented stellar disk heating diagnostics, including vertical scale-height evolution and full velocity-dispersion tracking.
	5.	Implemented component-resolved angular momentum tracking to quantify angular momentum redistribution during the merger.
	6.	Implemented total energy computation and energy time-series diagnostics for numerical stability and symplectic integrity assessment.
	7.	Implemented time-reversal (Loschmidt) experiments via velocity inversion and RMS phase-space separation metrics.
	8.	Developed a post-processing orchestration layer aggregating all diagnostics into a reproducible analysis pipeline.
	9.	Verified unit consistency, mass conservation, and internal data-flow integrity across all modules.

⸻

OUTCOME

A complete, research-grade simulation and analysis framework capable of quantifying macroscopic irreversibility, disk heating, angular momentum transport, and numerical fidelity in idealised galaxy merger experiments.

